      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "Nemonoxacin, a small ionizable amine-containing drug, has absolute oral bioavailability nearly 100% in healthy humans, demonstrating that complete absorption is achievable for small polar/basic molecules.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C23"],
      "claim": "Entecavir, a small polar nucleoside analogue with multiple polar groups, has absolute oral bioavailability of 100% in humans, supporting high oral absorption for small hydrophilic molecules.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C18"],
      "claim": "Minocycline, an ionizable amine-containing drug, exhibits 90–100% absolute oral bioavailability in humans, showing that basic amine drugs can be almost completely absorbed.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C29"],
      "claim": "Gabapentin, a small primary-amine-containing molecule with low molecular weight, has absolute oral bioavailability of about 60% in humans, indicating substantial absorption for small polar amines.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C32"],
      "claim": "Thalidomide, a small rigid molecule, has absolute oral bioavailability around 100% in healthy volunteers, supporting high oral bioavailability for small rigid structures.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a small rigid primary amine (amantadine). Multiple high-transfer human absolute bioavailability measurements for small polar/basic drugs show complete or near-complete oral absorption, including nemonoxacin ~100%, entecavir 100%, minocycline 90–100%, thalidomide ~100%, and gabapentin ~60%. These direct experimental results outweigh the uncertain physicochemical prior and support F >= 20%.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule in the supplied evidence.",
    "No query-specific data on intestinal permeability, first-pass metabolism, or transporter effects.",
    "The physicochemical prior is mixed and low confidence."
  ],
